    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d170">

                <pb facs="0589" n="467" xml:id="pg467"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr.
                            Lincoln.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1889-10-29"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 29,
                        1889</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 107.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department is gratified to learn by
                    your No. 101 of the 16th instant that the Canadian Government has directed the
                    non-enforcement, for an indefinite period, of the act of 1886 as to shipping
                    seamen before “shipping masters,” so far as American vessels are concerned; and
                    you will take occasion so to inform Her Majesty’s Government.</p>
                <p>As regards the discrimination against American vessels at Halifax in the matter
                    of pilotage dues, the Department can not doubt that the expected decision of the
                    authorities will be favorable.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">James G. Blaine.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
